INSERT INTO `mguide_course_1` VALUES (1,'nn305','2020-05-27 19:06:41','2017_2018','1',1,2,1,'','The point of this course is to understand _symmetries_, reversible transformations of an object. For example, reflections and rotations are the symmetries of an equilateral triangle. But, rather than studying the symmetries of a specific object like a triangle, we notice that the symmetries of _any_ object obey a few simple rules. And we have the clever idea of instead studying _anything_ that satisfies these rules - we invent an abstract object called a group where the _only_ thing we know is that it satisfies these simple rules, called axioms. This is a really powerful idea, it turns out we can deduce a lot from just these few rules, and this means we can understand the symmetries of _all_ objects better.\n\nThis is an example of a key insight to higher level maths, called abstraction. We want to understand an idea, like symmetries, observe that it satisfies a few simple rules, and instead study _any_ object that satisfies those rules. This idea is the key insight behind a field of maths known as abstract algebra, and group theory is your first introduction to this.\n\nIn practice, you\'ll spend the first half of this course exploring these simple rules, and proving theorems about general groups, and the second half analysing some specific important examples of groups. This may feel a bit abstract and ungrounded, but remember that you\'re really figuring out how to understand _all_ symmetries at the same time!\n','<p>The point of this course is to understand <em>symmetries</em>, reversible transformations of an object. For example, reflections and rotations are the symmetries of an equilateral triangle. But, rather than studying the symmetries of a specific object like a triangle, we notice that the symmetries of <em>any</em> object obey a few simple rules. And we have the clever idea of instead studying <em>anything</em> that satisfies these rules - we invent an abstract object called a group where the <em>only</em> thing we know is that it satisfies these simple rules, called axioms. This is a really powerful idea, it turns out we can deduce a lot from just these few rules, and this means we can understand the symmetries of <em>all</em> objects better.</p>\n<p>This is an example of a key insight to higher level maths, called abstraction. We want to understand an idea, like symmetries, observe that it satisfies a few simple rules, and instead study <em>any</em> object that satisfies those rules. This idea is the key insight behind a field of maths known as abstract algebra, and group theory is your first introduction to this.</p>\n<p>In practice, you\\\'ll spend the first half of this course exploring these simple rules, and proving theorems about general groups, and the second half analysing some specific important examples of groups. This may feel a bit abstract and ungrounded, but remember that you\\\'re really figuring out how to understand <em>all</em> symmetries at the same time!</p>\n','{\"Fun_score\": 4, \"Difficulty_score\": 3.5}');
