not actually that pure. Analysis contents are not that much, and you can find a lot of statistics and probability contents there.\n\nStochastic Financial Models uses Probability and Measure (P&M) **a lot**. I always heard Prof. Norris talking about monotone/dominated convergence theorems when proving results. Applied Probability and Principles of Statistics also mention P&M. When your lecturers talk about a theorem in P&M (and say it\'s non-examinable), it feels great if you already have a good understanding about it.\n\nIf you are a puremo doing analysis courses, P&M can teach you similar results from a different perspective. It\'s helpful for a mathematician to learn a result from different angles.\n\n# Is this course interesting?\nI would say yes! \n\nYou first learn some measure theory (as a foundational framework), then use it to define probability, independence, etc. Next, you learn some familiar things in a measure-theoretic way, such as Fourier transform and ergodicity. In particular, a lot of techniques and theorems learned before (like exchange between sum and integral, central limit theorem, strong law of large numbers, conditional expectation) will be rigorously proved in P&M (I think it\'s quite exciting)!\n\nProf. Breuillard taught this course in an intuitive way: He would first show you examples, and after fully exploring them, he presented the big general theorems (For example, the transition from Lebesgue measure to Carathéodory extension theorem). It made the abstract contents easier to understand.\n\n#Is this course hard?\nUnfortunately, I would say yes.\n\nIt\'s a new area of mathematics you haven\'t encountered in Part IA&IB, hence at the beginning it can take quite a long time to figure out what is actually going on (and that process is a bit painful). However, when you finally understand the results and can find the connections between them, it\'s just like discovering a new world! You will be proud of yourself mastering such deep and interesting materials, and also you will have a better understanding of some other Part II courses.\n\nIn terms of the exam difficulty, it depends. Some years\' questions are easy and other years\' questions might be hard. My attitude is to view P&M as a course to broaden my horizon, and hopefully I can get some marks (and even alphas) in the Tripos.\n\n#Useful resources\n1. Prof. E. Breuillard\'s notes on dpmms website:\nhttps://www.dpmms.cam.ac.uk/study/II/Probability%2BMeasure/\nNote that if he lectures this course next year, he will update the notes as the course goes on. This means the full version of notes might disappear, so download it now :)\n\n2. Prof. J. Norris\' notes on his personal website:\nhttp://www.statslab.cam.ac.uk/~james/Lectures/pm.pdf\nIt\'s good to have this version as a reference. Note that Prof. Norris tends to teach things in a very pure way, so this version might look a bit different from Prof. Breuillard\'s version.\n\nFinally, if you plan to take this course, I hope you enjoy it. :smile:','<h1 id=\\\"h1-is-this-course-useful-\\\"><a name=\\\"Is this course useful?\\\" class=\\\"reference-link\\\"></a><span class=\\\"header-link octicon octicon-link\\\"></span>Is this course useful?</h1><p>Although this sounds like a very pure course, it\\\'s not actually that pure. Analysis contents are not that much, and you can find a lot of statistics and probability contents there.</p>\n<p>Stochastic Financial Models uses Probability and Measure (P&amp;M) <strong>a lot</strong>. I always heard Prof. Norris talking about monotone/dominated convergence theorems when proving results. Applied Probability and Principles of Statistics also mention P&amp;M. When your lecturers talk about a theorem in P&amp;M (and say it\\\'s non-examinable), it feels great if you already have a good understanding about it.</p>\n<p>If you are a puremo doing analysis courses, P&amp;M can teach you similar results from a different perspective. It\\\'s helpful for a mathematician to l