r amount of technicalities which I found a bit difficult to get my head around at first.  Nevertheless, one has to bear in mind that this serves the purpose of building a big picture culminating in the Fundamental Theorem of Galois Theory. Overall, it is a cohesive course with deep new ideas.\n\n#Other thoughts on the course\nThe example sheets tend to be quite long. Don\'t expect too much trickery -- the questions mostly revolve around understanding the main results and knowing how to apply them. Even though the course can be a bit overwhelming while it\'s being lectured, once you get used to all the new concepts, the course material is not bad at all. The exam questions are usually quite standard, so they can be successfully tackled with enough practice and a good understanding of the material.\n\n#Prerequisites/related courses\nGRM is an essential prerequisite and Linear Algebra contains some useful ideas. I\'d definitely recommend doing Galois Theory if you intend to do Number Fields. Conversely, Number Fields might help provide motivation for some parts of Galois Theory, so it\'s a good follow-up to this course.\n\n#Who\'d enjoy the course?\nThe most closely related IB course is obviously GRM. In my opinion, both courses are very interesting, but in a different way. While I found most parts of GRM to be elegant but rather straightforward, more time and effort was needed for me to appreciate the beauty of Galois Theory. As a result, it\'s kind of hard to tell who\'d enjoy the course, but I\'d say it is a must for anyone inclined to algebra/number theory. In any case, this course is a classic and I\'d recommend it to anyone interested in pure maths.','<h1 id=\\\"h1-what-is-the-course-about-\\\"><a name=\\\"What is the course about?\\\" class=\\\"reference-link\\\"></a><span class=\\\"header-link octicon octicon-link\\\" style=\\\"user-select: auto;\\\"></span>What is the course about?</h1><p>Galois Theory is all about developing the language of field extensions a