y helpful and they try their best with coping with this interesting situation (and they are really open for feedback :)). Also, the lecturer treats this as a \'\'leisure\'\' course and sometimes decides to show us the proof that circle is optimal \'\'because you shouldn\'t get degree from Cambridge if you don\'t know it\'\' or randomly asking about greatest Czech composers, which is quite a fun interlude.\n\nHowever, example sheets sometimes seem to be touching slightly different topics to the things lectured. You should be able to do them just from lecture material but if you read through \"Combinatorics\" by Bela Bollobas, you\'ll be much better off with doing them. In general, it is a good book for most of the course.','<p>If you have done Olimpiad Maths when you were in high school, then what you expect under the term is almost exactly what you get in this course (if you haven\\\'t -- it\\\'s about playing around with discrete stuff a lot, probably the most similar in flavour among Part IA,IB,II courses are Numbers and Sets, Graph Theory, Coding and Cryptography, I would guess).</p>\n<p>It is just a collection of various short topics that the lecturer likes the most (if you\\\'ve seen in your old highschool days Nullstellensatz in combi, I would say nothing will surprise you -- it will be just a collection of nice, easy problems).</p>\n<p>On one hand, it\\\'s nice due to the fact that contrary to some Part III courses, it doesn\\\'t speed up heavily in the last third of the course. On the other, you won\\\'t have heavy results that are slowly built up. </p>\n<p>Another issue with this course is that there is quite a gap between those with previous olympic/combinatorial experience (to be fair, it\\\'s accessible for everyone, no matter of the background, compare with Numbers and Sets and difference between people with previous olympic and non-olympic experience) -- so there is a bit of a paradox going during the lectures -- the lecturer asks from time to time som