2019_2020','1',1,2,97,'','This course aims to describe mathematically how to solve inverse problems in imaging, such as reconstructing the image of a human body from the X-rays that have passed through. The course is broadly divided into two parts: the first solves inverse problems using tools from functional analysis while the second uses Bayesian probability theory. Although the course at first sounds applied in nature, it is almost entirely theoretical with only occasional references to how to apply these methods to images in practice. While the theory is still interesting, it was not exactly what I expected and I left the course unsure of how to apply the theory to solve inverse problems in the real world. Nevertheless, the course is well organised with a good set of lecture notes, and many of the fundamentals of functional analysis and probability theory are taught prior to their use, making the course relatively accessible even without a significant amount of prior knowledge. Additionally, even though the lectures could be dense at times, the example sheets and the exam focused on the most understandable portions of the material, making them doable even without a complete understanding of all the course material.','<p>This course aims to describe mathematically how to solve inverse problems in imaging, such as reconstructing the image of a human body from the X-rays that have passed through. The course is broadly divided into two parts: the first solves inverse problems using tools from functional analysis while the second uses Bayesian probability theory. Although the course at first sounds applied in nature, it is almost entirely theoretical with only occasional references to how to apply these methods to images in practice. While the theory is still interesting, it was not exactly what I expected and I left the course unsure of how to apply the theory to solve inverse problems in the real world. Nevertheless, the course is well organised with a good set of 